A stairway, with three steps, has three risers that are each $8$ inches high and three { treads that are each $10$ inches deep. What is the area, in square inches, of the side panel of the stairway?

$$\text{Solution:}$$ \\
To find the area of the side panel of the stairway, note that the outside edge of the bottom tread is at $x=0$, and the inside edge of the top tread is at $x=30$. 

The equation of the topmost point of the panel is $y=24$. This is either a horizontal line at $y=24$, or a diagonal line decreasing progressively to $y=24$.
Below this, there are effectively two horizontal lines (for the three, since each is counted twice for the sides of 3 pairs of vertical lines separating the three treads, thinking of this collection of lines in the staircase). The first bottom one, (with highest y-value of the bottom two horizontal lines), has y-value equal to $16$; the second lower line for  the bottom two horizontal lines most visible in cross-section y-value has y-value equal to $8$.
Since all treads and riser have equal horizontal width and there are 3 pairs in the stairway,  and vertical heights of risers are also all the same as the horizontal distances, then the whole width of  the wall is made up of horizontal lines and vertical line segments. The horizontal distances
between these horizontal line for the wall of the stairway is $8$, since lines at y=24, and y=16 or lower, effectively , -mean that when considering copies of each pair of a line and a rise area is looked at, that
lowered by risers  such that gives total width 30. 
Hence 3 intervals of width 8 giving 3 $\cdot 8= 24$ of the total height, up to the horizontal line at 24: these can be concatenated:  The 
the solution examines  the wall on just one vertical edge to simplify and to make it much simpler. They result in a series of horizontal lines when viewed integrated. and viewed vertically. If 
$0\le x\le 24$ then $x$ is expressible by $8k$, $$k=0,1,2,\dots,3$. Notice that the panels will be whatever numbers of segments make the whole height $24$ if interpreted as separate strips. This means if 2 strips make it $16, 1 more strip knocks it past that to 24, and there is no vertical horizontal segment required between specific horizontal lines other than closest-pair segment required between adjacent treads.\\

